nodecode-supermemory is a rewrite of supermemoryai's supermemoryai/hermes-supermemory:

  https://github.com/supermemoryai/hermes-supermemory
  commit 91405dd00fdf2a0b3515b25c25557598f6793299

MIT, Copyright (c) 2025 Nous Research (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's memory provider -- its Supermemory client calls
(documents.add, search.memories, profile, memories.forget), the four model tools
and the JSON schemas they advertise, the container tag and its sanitizing, the
entity context that steers extraction, the [role: x]...[x:end] turn layout, the
custom_id that buckets a session's turns into one document per four hours, and
the bounded retry buffer -- reimplemented in Common Lisp against Nodecode's
cell contract, with nlk:http speaking the same HTTPS endpoints. The harness
seams are Nodecode's: the plugin's config file became the cell's `supermemory`
section, its tools are nle:register-tool registrations, and its sync_turn hook
is an observer on the :frame point.

What was left out, and why: the system-prompt block and the prefetch of a
Supermemory block before each turn of the plugin are not carried, because
Nodecode's recall seam reads standing sections once per turn and carries no
per-message query -- the model calls supermemory_search instead, and (help
:supermemory) tells it when; and the kebab-case tool aliases (supermemory-save
and friends), because Nodecode's catalog has one name per tool and the four
snake_case names are the original's own. Also left out: the Hermes-side
`hermes memory setup` / `status` verbs (the section, /settings and
/supermemory are the same surface), the custom-container whitelist (one
container per cell section here), {identity} templates expanded from the
harness's agent identity, and the PostHog usage events the Python client
sends.
